content.text:
The `#playbackBar > div { flex: 1 1 calc(50% - 16px); min-width: 160px; }` applies to direct child divs. Now my `#playbackBar` has children: `.playback-handle` (a span), `.playbackBody` (a wrapper div), and the actual controls are now nested inside `.playbackBody`. So `#playbackBar > div` only matches `.playbackBody` (a single div) — not the individual controls inside it.

That's not ideal — I need the controls to lay out properly when sheet is open. My new CSS in the second 720px block has `.playbackBody { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; padding: 12px 0; }`. Good — that's a vertical column. Each control group inside remains as-is from desktop.

Let me also ensure the existing playbackBar child styles don't override mine. Since I'm setting `position: fixed` on `#playbackBar` and overriding `padding`/`gap`, the previous `padding: 8px 10px; gap: 8px;` would still apply. My new values are `padding: 12px 14px calc(16px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px)); gap: 10px;` — I should explicitly override. Looking back at my CSS:

```css
#playbackBar {
  position: fixed;
  ...
  padding: 12px 14px calc(16px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
  flex-direction: column;
  ...
}
```

These come AFTER the earlier `#playbackBar { padding: 8px 10px; gap: 8px; }` in the same query stack. Same specificity — last wins. Mine wins. 

